/-
Fmt/M2EngineLRw.lean: tests that the altered copies of one set of files of the second toy are
refused, and by which stage of the verifier.

The second toy is a test protocol of the programs. An altered copy is the honest proof with one byte
or eight bytes overwritten at one place, without its last byte, or with one byte more
(`f_altered_01` to `f_altered_13`, Fmt/M2ProgLRFiles.lean).

The set of files: tiny parameters with a repeated position. These are the tiny parameters (a size
meant for reading by eye; it reaches no security level) under another key of the prover's coins.
Three of the toy's rounds are named LOC, and each draws a list of positions. At that key the second
of the three draws one position twice. That round comes after a link with a kept message (a message
that the next link sends again). In Lean, `who` reads a file at `Theirs.specTiny`
(Fmt/M2Theirs.lean) and decides at the sizes `tinyPar` (Fmt/M2Types.lean).

Sources. This file was generated by a program from files in the archive
toy-files-made-by-the-cuts-tools.tgz (sha256
f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1; the archive is not in this
repository), which the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with this Lean code) made for the
second toy (a chain of three links; Fmt/M2Dec.lean says what it is). The data are made-up toy data:
no model and no weights. Taken from the archive: nothing directly: the files are those of
Fmt/M2ProgLRFiles.lean. The generating program stops unless every file that the archive's own list
of checksums (SUMS) names, the lists among them, has the sha256 given there. The file imports no
Mathlib.

The verifier on bytes has two stages. The first is the strict reader, `wholeChainF`
(Fmt/Rules8Fast.lean). It is this library's rendering of the format page's verifier up to its
decision: it cuts the file, recomputes the hash chain from which the challenges are drawn, tests the
proofs of work and checks every Merkle opening against its root. The second is the decision, the
algebraic part of the check: `Fmt.M2.dec` (Fmt/M2All.lean).

1. What the file contains. One helper, `who`, which gives the outcome: "reader" if the strict reader
   refuses the file, else "decision" if the decision refuses, else "accepted". And tests.

2. What is checked when the file compiles (#guard). File by file: refused by the strict reader. The
   archive has no false proofs for this set.

3. Theorems. None.

4. What is not claimed.
   No theorem. The tests (#guard) are evaluations that Lean makes when the file compiles. They speak
     of these bytes and of nothing else.
   No statement on soundness follows from these tests.
   The outcome does not tell which check of the strict reader, or which of the decision's two
     checks, failed. (The two are the checks of the last block of the proximity test. The decision
     also refuses where sizes or the shape of its input do not fit, so the outcome "decision" need
     not come from one of the two; see the head of Fmt/M2Dec.lean, under 1.)
   Nothing is claimed here of the compiled verifier (see Run/M2Engine*.lean).

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
import Fmt.M2Theirs
import Fmt.M2ProgLRFiles
import Fmt.M2All
import Fmt.M1View
import Fmt.Rules8Fast

namespace Fmt
namespace M2
namespace EngineLRw
open ProgLR Fmt.M1

/-- The outcome on the file `b`: the stage that refuses it, or "accepted". -/
def who (b : ByteArray) : String :=
  match wholeChainF Theirs.specTiny b with
  | none => "reader"
  | some o => if dec tinyPar (viewOf Theirs.specTiny.firstTree o) then "accepted" else "decision"

#guard who f_altered_01 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_02 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_03 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_04 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_05 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_06 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_07 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_08 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_09 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_10 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_11 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_12 = "reader"
#guard who f_altered_13 = "reader"

end EngineLRw
end M2
end Fmt
